  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 244 PM MDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nIt is a bit milder than previously forecast today, with \ntemperatures still in the 70s for much of the low elevations and \nonly a few 80s over the Palmer Divide. The cooler temperatures \nhave been in part due to the cold front that pushed through early \nthis morning, as well as a streak of high clouds that has moved in\nfrom NW to SE Colorado. Cumulus cloud cover is increasing over \nthe mountains with a few showers and thunderstorms already moving \nthrough the Front Range. Although moisture transport is not \nparticularly enhanced this afternoon, precipitable water (PW) \nvalues remain slightly above normal over the state. The better \nchance for rain this afternoon will reside over the high terrain, \nalthough we do not expect rainfall to amount more than a trace. \nFor the Denver metro and I-25 corridor, current soundings show a \nvery capped profile, which comes at no surprise given the cool \ntemperatures in place. However, any high-based showers or storms \nthat are able to move off of the foothills and over the plains \nwill be able to produce gusty winds up to 40 mph in its vicinity. \n\nUpper level ridging over Colorado returns for just a day tomorrow, \nmaking Sunday the warmest day of the week. Afternoon temperatures \nwill climb into the upper 80s and lower 90s over the Denver area and \nI-25 corridor. Portions of the northeast Colorado plains, such as \nPhillips and Sedgwick counties, may struggle to warm up due to \nlingering morning stratus keeping cooler and moister air locked near \nthe surface. A few factors will play into tomorrow's afternoon and \nevening shower potential. First, southwest flow aloft will begin to \nadvect monsoonal moisture into the region, with PW values increasing \nto >150% of normal by late afternoon. This uptick in moisture will \nbegin over western Colorado and gradually move east over the Front \nRange and plains. Model soundings over the Denver metro show good \nagreement in DCAPE values soaring into the 1200-1700 J/kg range and \na well-defined inverted-V sounding. Despite the dry low levels, high-\nbased showers and microbursts will be able to produce wind gusts up \nto 50 mph, even with little to no precipitation at the surface. A \nlee cyclone east of Denver could provide additional forcing and a\npush of higher dewpoints in the early evening hours. A second \nround of showers and storms will be possible over the mountains \nSunday night into the early morning hours of Monday. \n\nThe active weather pattern will continue Monday through Thursday as \nmonsoonal moisture transport intensifies and shower coverage becomes \nmore numerous, particularly over the high terrain. Cooler \ntemperatures are also in store this week, with afternoon highs only \nreaching the mid 70s to low 80s at low elevations, and 50s-60s for \nthe mountains. At this time, Tuesday looks to be the best chance for \nlocally heavy rainfall for both the mountains and low elevations. \nHowever, Wednesday and Thursday could also be quite unsettled and \nwet days if a shortwave tracks through northern Colorado, as some \nmodel guidance has suggested. Ensemble trends do appear bring \nquieter and dry weather for the weekend.",
